WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.043 --> 00:00:10.370
Producer: It's a Nicolas Cage movie.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.830
Stephen Moss: That doesn't
always bode well, does it?

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.420
My name is Steve Moss, and for 35 years

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.410
I was a senior inspector of air accidents

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.900
at the Air Accidents Investigation
Branch at Farnborough.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.470
You've asked me to view
various clips from movies

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.743
and critique how realistic they are.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:38.328
[explosion]

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.668
The major accidents that I've dealt with

00:00:44.668 --> 00:00:46.820
in those 35 years

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.520
started with the fire on the runway

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.253
at Manchester in 1985,

00:00:52.253 --> 00:00:55.110
then Lockerbie, Pan Am 103.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.840
I've never heard of electrical failures

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.980
causing anything like that.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.650
Normally, electrical
failures, if they happen,

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.780
are likely to lead to gradual smoke.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.660
They don't really result

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.318
in sort of explosions

00:01:10.318 --> 00:01:12.720
and fireballs and things like this.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.540
I've never heard of that.
Never heard of that.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.100
Because they're protected.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.110
They have circuit breakers and fuses

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.653
like you have in your car or a home.

00:01:22.613 --> 00:01:25.030
[screaming]

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.460
They would lose
consciousness very quickly,

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.770
within a few seconds.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.470
Authorities did say that it was likely,

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.857
if they're free-falling
out of the aircraft,

00:01:38.857 --> 00:01:40.440
that they would come to,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.720
but they would be so confused and tumbling

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.390
that they wouldn't know
what the hell was going on.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.145
Producer: And I guess for a realism rating

00:01:46.145 --> 00:01:47.440
for that one?

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.690
Stephen: Three.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.063
Firstly, the aircraft
wreckage looks very realistic.

00:02:06.063 --> 00:02:09.610
I like that wiring looms
are all hanging down.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.530
I like...that's the sort of thing you see,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.960
carpets, seats everywhere.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.899
Brad Pitt and the zombie

00:02:15.899 --> 00:02:18.810
are in different parts of the aircraft.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.810
Depending on the nature of the impact,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.400
you can have a greater
chance of surviving,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.780
but sometimes it's the rear
and sometimes it's the center,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.558
over the top of the wing,
because that, on any aircraft,

00:02:30.558 --> 00:02:33.140
that is a very strong
part of the structure.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.860
However, if there's a fire,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.460
you're also sitting right
over the fuel tanks.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:38.990
I wouldn't say there's necessarily

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.830
any great survival benefit
being in an aisle seat.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.957
If you've got an intact aircraft

00:02:45.957 --> 00:02:47.770
and there's a fire develops,

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.150
then it's nice to be on an aisle seat

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.770
rather than sort of jammed
up against the window.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.578
So, I don't think stats would,

00:02:55.578 --> 00:02:57.136
statistics would bear out

00:02:57.136 --> 00:03:01.480
that survivability is, in
an accident, in an impact,

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.830
is greater for aisle seats
than for a window seat.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:05.983
I'll give it eight.

00:03:08.499 --> 00:03:10.674
[passengers scream]

00:03:10.674 --> 00:03:12.310
[gun fires, glass breaks]

00:03:13.535 --> 00:03:15.566
I've wrote here, "What is going on?"

00:03:15.566 --> 00:03:17.140
[producer laughs]

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.840
I think it would simply
punch a hole in the window,

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.460
so you'd have a bullet-sized hole.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.250
What this thing appears to show

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:27.370
is that the window
disintegrates completely

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.160
and takes a bit of the aircraft structure.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.410
Well, again, that really wouldn't happen

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.760
because what's known as
the window belt line,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.710
so the part of the fuselage
that the window's mounting,

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.670
is extra strong for that reason,

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:45.500
because the holes that are
cut out for the windows

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.120
are an obvious weak point.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.570
So they make sure that
the aircraft structure

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.475
is beefed up in that area.

00:03:52.691 --> 00:03:53.974
[glass breaks]

00:03:55.666 --> 00:03:57.833
[people falling]

00:03:57.833 --> 00:03:59.356
They can run down.

00:04:00.373 --> 00:04:02.340
That's why if you see every time

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.148
the flight attendants move a trolley

00:04:06.148 --> 00:04:07.431
and they stop,

00:04:07.431 --> 00:04:09.570
first thing they do is
they put on the brakes

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.980
with their foot so that it doesn't slide.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.318
I'll give it four.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.005
[crash]

00:04:26.047 --> 00:04:28.660
I don't think there's
anything particularly wrong.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.510
I think that could happen

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:34.190
and that's likely the way that
it would if it did happen,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.510
or if it does happen.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.510
The oxygen masks, yes, that's fair enough.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.193
They're designed to do that

00:04:40.193 --> 00:04:42.068
if you have a depressurization

00:04:42.068 --> 00:04:44.950
that was extremely rapid depressurization.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.120
People tend to think that it
can happen with an impact.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.800
With a very heavy impact, vertical impact,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.299
sometimes they drop down,

00:04:54.299 --> 00:04:58.041
but their primary purpose is to drop down

00:04:58.041 --> 00:05:01.290
if the aircraft depressurizes at altitude.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.410
So, you know, all that's
to the visual effect.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:08.180
It's known in the trade
as the rubber jungle.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.930
Yeah, I'll give that an eight.

00:05:16.881 --> 00:05:18.529
[explosion]

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.500
Yes, well. [laughs]

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.540
Several things wrong with this clip.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.780
Bruce Willis seems to be able to reach

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.621
inside the structure of the aircraft

00:05:30.621 --> 00:05:31.737
and pull a pipe out,

00:05:31.737 --> 00:05:33.470
which starts fuel gushing out.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.640
I don't think that would
really be possible.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.310
Another misconception amongst filmmakers

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:44.070
is that jet fuel is incredibly flammable.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.300
It's not, it's the reverse,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.630
it's much less flammable than petrol.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.740
From the start, I don't
think that the flame

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:54.190
going from his cigarette
lighter along a trail

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.800
and then up onto the
aircraft would ever happen,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.210
certainly not in those low temperatures.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.620
There's something about Bruce
Willis films, isn't there?

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.778
Is it "Armageddon" where he goes up

00:06:04.778 --> 00:06:06.703
in a space shuttle to try...

00:06:06.703 --> 00:06:09.348
and that thing's bouncing
off rocks, you know,

00:06:09.348 --> 00:06:12.530
and they're the most
delicate thing in the world.

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:15.060
You know, a fleck of paint
could bring those down.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.304
Producer: If you had to
give that one a rating

00:06:16.304 --> 00:06:18.153
out of 10, what would you give it?

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:21.326
Stephen: Two.

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:27.702
[passengers yell]

00:06:27.702 --> 00:06:29.260
Ken: The elevator feels really stiff, sir.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:30.733
Whip: All right, don't force it.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.745
Stephen: He says "the
elevator" has gone stiff,

00:06:34.745 --> 00:06:36.100
as though it's a lift.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.625
Well, no, they're always referred to

00:06:38.625 --> 00:06:40.950
in the plural, elevators.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.758
They're the movable surfaces

00:06:42.758 --> 00:06:44.232
at the rear of the aircraft

00:06:44.232 --> 00:06:46.190
that are controlled by the pilots

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:48.150
to make the aircraft go up or go down

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.140
or pitch up and pitch down.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.031
Ken: Gear's down. I don't think

00:06:51.031 --> 00:06:52.281
hydraulics is the problem, sir.

00:06:52.281 --> 00:06:53.630
Whip: We need to dump the fuel.

00:06:58.037 --> 00:07:02.119
Stephen: In an emergency,
you always dump fuel.

00:07:02.119 --> 00:07:03.418
No, afraid not.

00:07:03.418 --> 00:07:04.834
That's the last thing on your mind.

00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:07.270
You'd be trying to get the
aircraft under control.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.360
Dumping fuel isn't going to help you.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.970
The reason that long-haul
aircraft might dump fuel

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.280
is when they've got lots of time

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.600
to prepare for an emergency landing.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:19.810
In an emergency like this,

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:22.290
where they're struggling
to control the aircraft,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.645
dumping fuel wouldn't even
enter into their heads.

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:27.550
Ken: Oh, Lord Jesus, we're inverted!

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:28.640
Whip: We're fine, listen, we're level.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.430
We can maintain altitude like this.

00:07:31.422 --> 00:07:32.988
[plane roars]

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:35.980
Stephen: Rolling an airliner inverted

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:37.950
and flying it inverted,

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:39.110
I really don't think so.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:41.450
I'm not sure the structure
would actually withstand.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:44.488
You'd break a wing or something

00:07:44.488 --> 00:07:47.480
because they're not meant
to fly under negative G.

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.230
Whip: Now we gotta
revert to manual control.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:50.520
This side first.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.495
Ken: OK, got it.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.690
Nothing, no control.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.398
Stephen: The scenario of this

00:07:56.398 --> 00:07:58.731
is a little bit obscure.

00:07:58.731 --> 00:08:01.858
He talks about hydraulic failure

00:08:01.858 --> 00:08:04.320
and then talks about going to manual.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.860
He then has this big handle
that he pulls in select,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.750
and it gives him manual control.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.965
Most modern aircraft,

00:08:11.965 --> 00:08:15.048
it wouldn't be possible
to fly them in manual,

00:08:15.048 --> 00:08:16.256
and bigger aircraft,

00:08:16.256 --> 00:08:18.663
you can't fly them
manually. They're too heavy.

00:08:19.916 --> 00:08:20.749
Automated voice: 20.

00:08:20.749 --> 00:08:23.492
Whip: Brace. Brace for impact.

00:08:23.492 --> 00:08:25.492
[crash]

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.520
Stephen: Seems to be
relatively straightforward,

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:32.210
what's known as a deadstick landing.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.310
In other words, no engine's operating.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:36.643
And it's probably nose high.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:42.236
So he'd make the landing like that

00:08:42.236 --> 00:08:43.887
to try and touch down,

00:08:43.887 --> 00:08:46.680
so the cockpit would be
well clear of the ground.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.120
So, it's only if they ran into something,

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:50.561
a solid object, you know,

00:08:50.561 --> 00:08:52.702
a building or something like that,

00:08:52.702 --> 00:08:55.750
that the cockpit would be compromised.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:57.750
But the scenario doesn't
really hang together,

00:08:57.750 --> 00:08:59.833
so I'll give that a five.

00:09:12.206 --> 00:09:13.573
Of the clips I've seen,

00:09:13.573 --> 00:09:16.210
that, I think, is most realistic.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.850
I think that is what would happen.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.690
It's quite well done, the graphics.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:22.300
What happens next, though,

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:25.246
I think is perhaps not so realistic.

00:09:26.538 --> 00:09:28.880
John: Hey, hey!

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.210
Stephen: Now, if we look at the wreckage,

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:35.370
the term we used to use was "disrupted."

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:37.460
It's very badly broken up.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.310
As he runs in, he keeps
coming across passengers,

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:42.290
like this person on fire.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.060
I don't think anybody
would've survived the impact.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.520
I notice an engine is still winding down.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.445
Yes, maybe, but I think the engine

00:09:50.445 --> 00:09:52.536
would be pretty much battered

00:09:52.536 --> 00:09:55.611
and jammed by the impact.

00:09:55.611 --> 00:09:56.918
I'll give that eight.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.256
Sully: Birds.

00:10:00.256 --> 00:10:01.478
Jeff: Whoa.

00:10:01.478 --> 00:10:04.645
[thuds]

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:11.846
Stephen: When you
certify an engine design,

00:10:11.846 --> 00:10:13.496
a new engine design,

00:10:13.496 --> 00:10:15.910
they actually fire birds at it

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:18.330
of a specified weight and speed

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:21.029
and check that the
engine doesn't break up.

00:10:22.071 --> 00:10:24.530
It's gonna get damaged,
but what they don't want

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:26.235
is for it to stop.

00:10:26.235 --> 00:10:28.018
[engines dying]

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:31.758
Sully: Ignition start.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.680
Stephen: It sort of
gives you the impression

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:38.450
that the engines have failed completely.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:39.600
I don't think they did.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:42.070
I think they weren't
delivering enough power

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:44.564
for him to remain airborne,

00:10:44.564 --> 00:10:46.302
and I think there would
have been a lot of...

00:10:46.302 --> 00:10:48.330
it all goes very quiet and very still.

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.320
I think there would have
been a lot of vibration.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:52.380
I mean, it is realistic
and then it should be,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.358
because I think it's
probably one of the most

00:10:54.358 --> 00:10:57.190
documented sequence of events

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.033
of recent years, isn't it.

00:10:59.033 --> 00:11:03.112
One of the things that I
didn't like about this film

00:11:03.112 --> 00:11:05.610
wasn't the technical side of it,

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.770
it was the totally invented antipathy

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:11.660
from the National
Transportation Safety Board

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:13.290
and Sully himself.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.120
In other words, they
come across as bullying

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:19.460
and accusative that he
didn't do the right thing.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.450
That was a complete invention.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.761
I know, I believe Sullenberger himself

00:11:24.761 --> 00:11:27.061
said that that was not,

00:11:27.061 --> 00:11:29.469
that the NTSB actually supported him.

00:11:29.469 --> 00:11:33.080
He famously was in a
film called "Cast Away,"

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:34.480
where there is an air crash.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:36.060
He, I think, is the only survivor.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:38.070
That was quite a bit unrealistic.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.098
You know, with engines continuing to run,

00:11:40.098 --> 00:11:41.610
and at one point he's in danger

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:44.480
of being sucked into this
engine that was still running

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:46.115
even though it was in water.

00:11:46.115 --> 00:11:48.332
Oh, I think it's gotta be at least a nine.

00:11:49.842 --> 00:11:53.025
Howard: OK, set course for

00:11:53.025 --> 00:11:55.250
090.

00:11:56.354 --> 00:11:57.339
Preparing to descend.

00:11:57.339 --> 00:11:58.526
[engines fail]

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:03.900
Stephen: Maybe there's
documentary evidence that says

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:08.030
that Howard Hughes did
fly in shirtsleeves,

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.888
without a seat belt,

00:12:09.888 --> 00:12:12.988
in what looks like a car
seat with a headrest,

00:12:12.988 --> 00:12:16.561
in what appears to be
quite a nice, sizable room.

00:12:16.561 --> 00:12:18.387
I mean, this is supposed
to be a fighter, isn't it,

00:12:18.387 --> 00:12:20.850
a prototype fighter or a fighter bomber.

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:22.100
They're incredibly compact.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.520
You see bits of the structure,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.890
there's pipes and wires everywhere.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.720
I really would very much doubt

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:32.332
if the prototype of a new combat aircraft

00:12:32.332 --> 00:12:36.292
was as roomy and uncluttered as that.

00:12:36.292 --> 00:12:38.947
Howard: Aah! I'm going down!

00:12:38.947 --> 00:12:41.447
[crash]

00:12:44.671 --> 00:12:47.004
[explosion]

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:51.993
Stephen: Now, if we look at the wing route

00:12:51.993 --> 00:12:53.126
here that's visible,

00:12:53.126 --> 00:12:54.690
you can see a dent in it.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.530
But in the other shots of it,

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:57.921
it's pretty much intact

00:12:57.921 --> 00:13:00.447
apart from the bits it's lost.

00:13:00.447 --> 00:13:03.555
Not too sure about the landing gear

00:13:03.555 --> 00:13:06.733
running across the roof.

00:13:06.733 --> 00:13:09.870
Producer: Did you have a
realism mark for this one?

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:11.161
Stephen: Six.

00:13:17.163 --> 00:13:19.504
[metal clanking]

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:28.185
I'm intrigued about what these devices are

00:13:28.185 --> 00:13:30.720
that they're fastening into the fuselage.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:33.460
I think the first time you
tried to put any weight on this

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.230
or to put any pull on
it, those would pull out.

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:38.190
There's not a lot holding
them in the fuselage.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:39.520
They'd just give way.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:43.410
Assuming it's a real airborne
sequence and not CGI.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.040
It must have been pre-prepared.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.570
So, yes, you can do a shot like this,

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.260
but the aircraft's probably got

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:52.568
strengthened rigging inside it,

00:13:52.568 --> 00:13:55.326
an invisible rigid ring.

00:13:55.326 --> 00:13:56.420
I would have given it a five,

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:57.760
but I will give it one for the fact

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:00.926
that it is a well-prepared stunt.

00:14:03.873 --> 00:14:05.240
[passengers scream]

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.810
The sound of the engines is all wrong.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:15.130
They have recorded a twin
piston engine aircraft,

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:17.088
which makes that sort of

00:14:17.088 --> 00:14:20.621
growling dive-bomber-type noise.

00:14:20.621 --> 00:14:24.026
This aircraft is a turboprop,

00:14:24.026 --> 00:14:26.729
which makes a sort of more like a whine

00:14:26.729 --> 00:14:27.778
'cause it's got propellers.

00:14:27.778 --> 00:14:29.986
It makes, they all make the same sound,

00:14:29.986 --> 00:14:31.452
and that's not the case.

00:14:31.452 --> 00:14:33.830
This was quite well known at the time,

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:35.540
a quite famous accident at the time,

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:39.430
because some people survived
for, I think it was 72 days,

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:41.210
in those freezing conditions,

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:43.905
and I'm afraid the macabre bit

00:14:43.905 --> 00:14:46.197
was that they actually had to

00:14:46.197 --> 00:14:49.000
resort to cannibalism to survive.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.150
But of course it was cannibalism

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.241
of those who died in the accident.

00:14:52.241 --> 00:14:54.283
[plane engines roaring]

00:14:54.283 --> 00:14:55.487
[beeping]

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:01.900
There's a warning light
flashing that says "pull up."

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:03.241
I'm pretty certain that

00:15:03.241 --> 00:15:06.491
that would not have been
fitted to this aircraft

00:15:06.491 --> 00:15:10.350
at the period of 1972, was it? 1973?

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:13.730
It's a system called Ground
Proximity Warning System,

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:18.120
and it was developed and mandated in 1974.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:20.250
I think they're getting it confused

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:23.657
with what is known as a stall warning,

00:15:23.657 --> 00:15:26.710
because the pilot is
heaving back on the stick

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:28.132
to try and avoid the mountain.

00:15:28.132 --> 00:15:32.268
He would probably be in danger
of stalling the aircraft.

00:15:32.268 --> 00:15:33.492
[crash]

00:15:33.492 --> 00:15:35.500
[passengers scream]

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:43.340
One of the first things to note

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:46.280
is the whole sequence
is lasting far too long.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:48.390
I timed it from first impact

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:51.633
with the mountain where the tail came off

00:15:51.633 --> 00:15:53.750
until it came to rest,
and it was a minute.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:56.880
Another very common movie misconception,

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:58.570
because I guess they just want

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:00.910
to stretch out the action a bit.

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:02.050
You know, the real thing

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:04.160
probably would have been
over in a few seconds.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:06.200
And certainly this massive ski jump

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:08.010
that the aircraft performs,

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:11.330
and it lands, it's still
intact, it's not even deformed.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:14.443
It would have been a
massive, crushing blow.

00:16:14.443 --> 00:16:17.151
I'd give it six out of 10.

00:16:19.174 --> 00:16:20.207
[crash]

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:32.860
I've written here, "Like this one."

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:35.360
A water landing is a ditching.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.580
Lot of media get that wrong.

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:40.250
They talk about, "The
aircraft ditched on land."

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:44.820
Ditching is purposely
executed landing on water.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.850
It depends on the type of aircraft.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.590
The "Sully" film showed
that it can be done

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:52.570
with a modern airline.

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:55.958
It's one of the very few
cases with a modern airliner

00:16:55.958 --> 00:16:57.660
actually successfully ditching.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:16:59.738
It's not the worst thing that can happen.

00:16:59.738 --> 00:17:03.930
I happen to know that the B-24 Liberator

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:06.310
was a difficult aircraft to ditch.

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:08.250
It had a habit of breaking into two.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.620
The poor old pilots were the least likely

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:12.220
I think to get out.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:15.270
I don't think the gunners
would stay in their turrets

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:16.940
if they know they're going to ditch.

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:19.920
They would take up a position

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:22.540
probably somewhere around
the middle of the aircraft.

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:24.522
I'm gonna give it nine.

00:17:24.522 --> 00:17:26.516
I've wrote here, "What is going on?"

00:17:26.516 --> 00:17:28.616
[producer laughs]

